            <ArticleTitle>Cryptogenic left main thrombosis: successful mechanical clot retrieval with a self-expanding trapping device.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>553-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/ccd.25162</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We present the case of a 52-year-old male with ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction due to a spontaneous large left main thrombosis, without any angiographic evidence for coronary artery disease. After multiple unsuccessful attempts of thrombaspiration the large clot was mechanically retrieved by a flow restoration device that was primarily made for intracranial interventions. Intravascular ultrasound revealed marginal lumen narrowing after the intervention, but the final coronary angiogram showed a patent left main and there was no relevant stenosis remaining.</AbstractText>
